From cfcefb06a56776bbb574fc4394a5e31ed82b96f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Benjamin Larsson <banan@ludd.ltu.se>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:32:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: airoha: ignore erased EN7570 APD anchor voltages

The APD voltage-to-code conversion picks its four-segment piecewise fit when
both anchor words have a populated upper half. Erased flash reads as all
ones, which satisfies that test and then decodes to four identical anchors of
6553.5V, so the monotonic check that follows fails and the driver declines to
write the DAC.

A board whose factory data omits the anchors is therefore left with no APD
bias programmed at all, costing receiver sensitivity, and reports an APD slope
error that suggests corrupt calibration rather than absent calibration.

Treat the erased pattern as absent, as the monitor-photodiode targets in this
same file already do, so those boards fall back to the legacy single-slope
conversion.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt Project <openwrt-devel@lists.openwrt.org>
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/airoha/en7570_txrx.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/airoha/en7570_txrx.c b/drivers/net/phy/airoha/en7570_txrx.c
index 21b2a5343613..f2dd97a434eb 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/airoha/en7570_txrx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/airoha/en7570_txrx.c
@@ -354,7 +354,15 @@ static int en7570_apd_voltage_to_code(struct en7570_priv *priv, s32 v_mv)
 	u32 w2 = lddla_flash_read(&priv->lddla, EN7570_FL_APD_VOLTAGE_2);
 	s32 step, code;
 
-	if ((w1 & 0xffff0000) && (w2 & 0xffff0000)) {
+	/*
+	 * Erased flash reads as all ones, which passes a test for the upper
+	 * half being populated and then decodes as four identical 6553.5V
+	 * anchors. Treat it as absent so a board whose factory data omits the
+	 * anchors falls back to the legacy slope instead of leaving the APD
+	 * bias unprogrammed.
+	 */
+	if (w1 != EN7570_FLASH_ERASED && w2 != EN7570_FLASH_ERASED &&
+	    (w1 & 0xffff0000) && (w2 & 0xffff0000)) {
 		/* Four-segment piecewise-linear over 64 codes per segment. */
 		s32 v00 = (s32)(w1 >> 16) * 100;
 		s32 v40 = (s32)(w1 & 0xffff) * 100;
-- 
2.55.0

